; =====================================================================
; structures.derived.cddl -- DERIVED. This file is NOT verbatim.
;
; The verbatim transcription of RFC 9052's CDDL is cose.cddl. The two
; files are kept strictly apart: nothing here should be quoted as the
; text of RFC 9052 (STD 96).
;
; Scope: a self-contained CDDL for the three structures this project
; exports -- COSE_Sign1, Sig_structure, COSE_Key -- with every rule
; they reference resolved in this file, so that the file can be fed to
; a CDDL tool on its own and validates standalone.
;
; Source sections of RFC 9052 (STD 96):
;   §1.4  CDDL Grammar for CBOR Data Structures  (entry-point convention)
;   §1.5  CBOR-Related Terminology               (label, values)
;   §3    Header Parameters                      (Headers, header_map,
;                                                 empty_or_serialized_map)
;   §3.1  Common COSE Header Parameters          (Generic_Headers, Table 3)
;   §4.2  Signing with One Signer                (COSE_Sign1,
;                                                 COSE_Sign1_Tagged)
;   §4.4  Signing and Verification Process       (Sig_structure, and the
;                                                 prose constraints on it)
;   §7    Key Objects                            (COSE_Key)
;   §7.1  COSE Key Common Parameters             (Table 4, Table 5)
;   §9    CBOR Encoding Restrictions             (encoding notes below)
;
; How this differs from cose.cddl -- the whole of the delta:
;   1. Only the export-relevant rules are carried over; COSE_Sign,
;      COSE_Signature, the COSE_Encrypt / COSE_Mac families,
;      COSE_Messages, COSE_KeySet and the other tag wrappers are not.
;   2. Rules are re-indented to column 0 with a uniform 4-space body
;      indent. RFC 9052 sets them in a 3-space text margin and is not
;      internally consistent about the body indent (§4 uses 4 spaces,
;      §6.1/§6.2 use 3, §6.3 uses 5).
;   3. A derived entry-point rule, Exported_Types, is added so that a
;      tool whose root is the first rule can reach all of the exported
;      structures. RFC 9052 §1.4 uses `start` and `Internal_Types` for
;      the same purpose; those rules are not reused here, because their
;      right-hand sides name rules this file deliberately omits.
;   4. Sig_structure_strict and its two arms are added. They are the
;      §4.4 *prose* expressed in CDDL; see the note on them below.
;      RFC 9052 §1.4 states that its CDDL is informational and its
;      prose is normative, so the strict rule is the closer reading of
;      the normative text -- but it is still ours, not the RFC's.
;      Every constraint it carries traces to a requirement id; the
;      ids are cited at each line of the two arms below.
;   5. Comments are rewritten. Inline value comments that were copied
;      from the RFC's fragments keep the RFC's wording; everything
;      marked NOTE, DANGLING or DERIVED is ours.
;
; Nothing is invented. Where RFC 9052 leaves a value space to RFC 9053
; or to an IANA registry, the place is marked "; DANGLING:" and the
; type is left exactly as loose as RFC 9052 leaves it.
; =====================================================================

; --- DERIVED entry point (see delta item 3) ---------------------------
; First rule in the file, so a CDDL tool that roots validation at the
; first rule can reach every exported structure.
Exported_Types = COSE_Sign1_Tagged
              / COSE_Sign1
              / Sig_structure
              / Sig_structure_strict
              / COSE_Key

; --- §1.5 primitives --------------------------------------------------
label = int / tstr
values = any

; --- §3 header buckets ------------------------------------------------
; NOTE (§3): both buckets are always present. `protected` is the CBOR
; encoding of a header_map wrapped in a bstr; a zero-length bstr means
; "empty", and senders SHOULD prefer it over an encoded empty map
; (h'a0'), which recipients MUST nonetheless accept. CDDL cannot state
; the SHOULD, and cannot state the §3 requirement that a label MUST NOT
; appear twice in one map or (SHOULD) in both buckets of one layer.
; Ids: both buckets present rfc-9052#R-0009, sender preference
; rfc-9052#R-0007, recipient acceptance rfc-9052#R-0008, label
; uniqueness rfc-9052#R-0011 with rfc-9052#R-0012 for the rejection,
; cross-bucket check rfc-9052#R-0013, protected empty when not
; cryptographically covered rfc-9052#R-0006.
Headers = (
    protected : empty_or_serialized_map,
    unprotected : header_map
)

header_map = {
    Generic_Headers,
    ; DANGLING (§3, §11.1): every header parameter other than the six
    ; below comes from the IANA "COSE Header Parameters" registry; the
    ; algorithm-specific ones are defined in RFC 9053. RFC 9052 defines
    ; no CDDL rule for them, so the wildcard below stays a wildcard.
    * label => values
}

empty_or_serialized_map = bstr .cbor header_map / bstr .size 0

; --- §3.1 common header parameters (Table 3) --------------------------
; The inline names on the right are RFC 9052's own.
; DANGLING (§3.1): the *values* of labels 1 and 3 are registry-defined.
; Label 1 (alg) takes values from the IANA "COSE Algorithms" registry,
; populated by RFC 9053; label 3 (content type) takes a CoAP
; Content-Format integer or a media-type text string. RFC 9052 gives
; only the CBOR types below, and so does this file.
Generic_Headers = (
    ? 1 => int / tstr,  ; algorithm identifier
    ? 2 => [+label],    ; criticality
    ? 3 => tstr / int,  ; content type
    ? 4 => bstr,        ; key identifier
    ? ( 5 => bstr //    ; IV
        6 => bstr )     ; Partial IV
)

; --- §4.2 COSE_Sign1 --------------------------------------------------
; NOTE (§4.2): `payload` is nil for detached content. A zero-length bstr
; payload is a distinct case: §4.1 gives it to signatures with message
; recovery, where the recovered bytes are removed from the payload.
COSE_Sign1 = [
    Headers,
    payload : bstr / nil,
    signature : bstr
]

COSE_Sign1_Tagged = #6.18(COSE_Sign1)

; --- §4.4 Sig_structure ----------------------------------------------
; Same shape as the §4.4 fragment: `sign_protected` is optional and the
; context string is unconstrained relative to it.
Sig_structure = [
    context : "Signature" / "Signature1",
    body_protected : empty_or_serialized_map,
    ? sign_protected : empty_or_serialized_map,
    external_aad : bstr,
    payload : bstr
]

; DERIVED (delta item 4): the §4.4 fragment above accepts
; ["Signature1", body, sign, aad, payload] and ["Signature", body, aad,
; payload], both of which §4.4's prose forbids -- prose item 3 says the
; signer-protected field "is omitted for the COSE_Sign1 signature
; structure", and prose item 1 ties "Signature1" to COSE_Sign1 and
; "Signature" to COSE_Signature. The two arms below tie the context
; string to the arity. Validate exports against this rule; quote the
; rule above as RFC 9052's.
;
; Provenance of every constraint in the two arms, so that nothing here
; is taken on the extractor's word (ids are requirements.yaml entries;
; the corresponding verbatim prose is in normative.md under §4.4):
;   context "Signature"  for a COSE_Signature        rfc-9052#R-0034
;   context "Signature1" for a COSE_Sign1            rfc-9052#R-0035
;   body_protected is the body's protected bstr,
;     zero-length when there are no protected attrs  rfc-9052#R-0036
;   sign_protected is the signer's protected bstr,
;     zero-length when empty, and OMITTED for
;     COSE_Sign1 -- both arms of the biconditional
;     come from this one entry                       rfc-9052#R-0037
;   external_aad is a bstr, zero-length by default   rfc-9052#R-0038
;   payload is the full payload as a bstr            rfc-9052#R-0039
;   the structure is created and populated before
;     it is encoded                                  rfc-9052#R-0040
; The arity binding is the composition of R-0034/R-0035 with R-0037 and
; adds nothing beyond them: R-0037 both forbids the field for COSE_Sign1
; and, by saying a zero-length byte string is used when there are no
; protected attributes, requires it otherwise. No constraint in either
; arm lacks an id above.
Sig_structure_strict = Sig_structure_Signature / Sig_structure_Signature1

; For a signature carried in a COSE_Signature inside a COSE_Sign.
Sig_structure_Signature = [
    context : "Signature",                      ; R-0034
    body_protected : empty_or_serialized_map,   ; R-0036
    sign_protected : empty_or_serialized_map,   ; R-0037 (present here)
    external_aad : bstr,                        ; R-0038
    payload : bstr                              ; R-0039
]

; For a signature carried in a COSE_Sign1. No signer-protected bucket.
Sig_structure_Signature1 = [
    context : "Signature1",                     ; R-0035
    body_protected : empty_or_serialized_map,   ; R-0036
                                                ; R-0037 (omitted here)
    external_aad : bstr,                        ; R-0038
    payload : bstr                              ; R-0039
]

; NOTE (§9): Sig_structure is one of the three structures whose encoding
; is restricted -- definite lengths only, and arguments encoded in the
; minimum possible length (so 1 is 0x01, never 0x1801). That is an
; encoder rule, not a data-model rule, and no CDDL in RFC 9052 or here
; expresses it. §4.3 adds that `external_aad` defaults to a zero-length
; byte string when the application supplies nothing, and §4.4 that
; `payload` is always the full payload regardless of transport.
; Ids: scope rfc-9052#R-0088, definite lengths rfc-9052#R-0089,
; minimum-length arguments rfc-9052#R-0090; the external_aad default is
; rfc-9052#R-0038 and the full-payload rule rfc-9052#R-0039.
;
; NOTE: the byte string that results from encoding a Sig_structure is
; named ToBeSigned by §4.4 (rfc-9052#R-0041), and RFC 9052 gives it no
; CDDL rule -- it is a value, not a data structure. The same holds for
; ToBeMaced (§6.3) and the AAD (§5.3). Their absence from both CDDL
; files is deliberate; they are modelled in messages.yaml instead.

; --- §7 COSE_Key ------------------------------------------------------
; The inline names on the right are RFC 9052's own.
; NOTE (§7): kty (label 1) is required; that is the only presence
; requirement RFC 9052 states for a COSE_Key.
; Ids: kty required rfc-9052#R-0059 (§7) and rfc-9052#R-0060 (§7.1);
; kty checked against the algorithm rfc-9052#R-0061 and carried into the
; trust decision rfc-9052#R-0062; alg, when present, MUST match the
; algorithm in use rfc-9052#R-0063 or the key MUST NOT be used
; rfc-9052#R-0064. None of those four is expressible in CDDL.
; NOTE (§7.1, Table 5): key_ops (label 4) values are defined in this
; source -- 1 sign, 2 verify, 3 encrypt, 4 decrypt, 5 wrap key,
; 6 unwrap key, 7 derive key, 8 derive bits, 9 MAC create,
; 10 MAC verify -- but RFC 9052's own CDDL does not enumerate them, so
; neither does this file. Algorithms decide which may appear.
; DANGLING (§7, §7.1, §11.2): the values of label 1 (kty) come from the
; IANA "COSE Key Types" registry and label 3 (alg) from "COSE
; Algorithms", both populated by RFC 9053; the key-type-specific
; parameters that the wildcard admits come from the IANA "COSE Key Type
; Parameters" registry, also RFC 9053. None of them has a CDDL rule in
; RFC 9052.
COSE_Key = {
    1 => tstr / int,          ; kty
    ? 2 => bstr,              ; kid
    ? 3 => tstr / int,        ; alg
    ? 4 => [+ (tstr / int) ], ; key_ops
    ? 5 => bstr,              ; Base IV
    * label => values
}
